────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 Why JPA?

- Work with Java objects instead of writing JDBC boilerplate every time
- Maps relationships (@OneToMany, @ManyToOne, …)
- Portable API — swap provider with less code change
- Fits Spring Boot via Spring Data JPA (repositories on top of JPA)

────────────────────────────────────────
How it fits in Spring Boot

Your code (@Entity, JpaRepository, JPQL)
        ↓
   Spring Data JPA
        ↓
   EntityManager (JPA API)
        ↓
   Hibernate (provider)
        ↓
   JDBC / HikariCP
        ↓
   Database

────────────────────────────────────────
Core JPA concepts (interview)

| Concept              | Meaning                                      |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Entity               | Java class mapped to a table                 |
| Persistence Context  | Cache of managed entities (L1) for a unit of work |
| EntityManager        | API to persist / find / merge / remove / flush |
| JPQL                 | Object-oriented query language (not SQL tables) |
| Provider             | Implementation that runs the real SQL        |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Key JPA Annotations

| Annotation        | Purpose                                      |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `@Entity`         | Marks class as persistent entity             |
| `@Table`          | Table name (optional; default = class name)  |
| `@Id`             | Primary key                                  |
| `@GeneratedValue` | Auto-generate PK (IDENTITY, SEQUENCE, …)     |
| `@Column`         | Column mapping (name, nullable, length, …)   |
| `@OneToMany`      | One → many relationship                      |
| `@ManyToOne`      | Many → one relationship                      |
| `@OneToOne`       | One → one                                    |
| `@ManyToMany`     | Many → many                                  |
| `@JoinColumn`     | FK column on the owning side                 |
| `@Transient`      | Field NOT persisted                          |
| `@Embedded` / `@Embeddable` | Value object mapped into same table |

────────────────────────────────────────
Mini example

@Entity
@Table(name = "employee")
public class Employee {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "dept_id")
    private Department department;
}

────────────────────────────────────────
EntityManager vs Spring Data (quick)

EntityManager (JPA):
entityManager.persist(emp);
entityManager.find(Employee.class, 1L);
entityManager.merge(emp);
entityManager.remove(emp);

Spring Data JPA (convenience on top):
employeeRepository.save(emp);      // persist or merge
employeeRepository.findById(1L);   // find
employeeRepository.delete(emp);    // remove

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Short Interview Answer

JPA is a Java specification for ORM — how entities map to relational DB tables.
It is not an implementation; Hibernate is the common provider that implements JPA.
In Spring Boot we mostly use JPA annotations and Spring Data repositories,
while Hibernate underneath talks to the database via JDBC.
